/*
 * The standard library, as an ordinary module manifest.
 *
 * There is nothing privileged about this file. `echoc` adds it to every build unless `--no-stdlib` says
 * otherwise, and from there the stdlib is one module among however many the invocation names - which is
 * what makes it the honest test of the module system: if a library cannot be expressed this way, neither
 * can this one.
 *
 * One pattern per directory rather than a single `**` sweep. Two directories must never be picked up and
 * naming them by inclusion is what keeps them out for a reason rather than by a skip list: `build/` holds
 * the generated embedded header rather than source, and `sketches/` is for Echo that describes a type the
 * language cannot express yet - the directory name is what says it does not compile. Adding a file to an
 * existing directory needs no edit here; adding a directory does, which is the right way round.
 *
 * **The directories are the two tiers.** `core/` is the language's own vocabulary - the types the compiler
 * binds with `#[core:]`, the builtins, and the namespaces those are built out of (`contract`, `mem`, `str`,
 * `arr`). `std/` is ordinary utility with nothing to do with the language, and everything in it is under
 * the `std` namespace. Which tier a name is in is visible in how you spell it, so the split is not
 * bookkeeping: it is the answer to "where does this new thing go".
 */

#[module: "stdlib"]

#[sources: "core/*.eco"]
#[sources: "std/math/*.eco"]
#[sources: "std/env/*.eco"]
#[sources: "std/io/*.eco"]
#[sources: "std/thread/*.eco"]
#[sources: "std/time/*.eco"]

// glibc before 2.34 keeps pthread out of libc. Darwin's libpthread is libSystem.
// `runtime: process` is the JIT half: there is no loadable `libpthread.so` on
// modern Linux, and the symbols are already mapped. Native `build` still gets `-lpthread`.
// Windows has no libpthread; std::thread talks to Win32 and the static-once helper
// names GetCurrentThreadId.
//
// glibc also keeps libm out of libc. LLVM lowers some math intrinsics (tanh,
// atan2) to libcalls, so a native `build` needs `-lm`. Darwin's libm is
// libSystem; `-lm` still works. `runtime: process` is the JIT half again:
// echoc is itself linked against libm, and `--target-os linux` on Windows
// must not try to open `libm.dll`. Windows UCRT already has the symbols;
// lld-link looking for m.lib is a missing-library error.
#[if: os != windows]
#[link: lib { name: "pthread", runtime: process }]
#[link: lib { name: "m", runtime: process }]
#[end]
